WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.589
Production XML workflows need operational visibility without exposing the data they process.

2
00:00:06.589 --> 00:00:17.905
XML documents can contain credentials, tokens, personal data, partner identifiers, internal routing values, private configuration, or signed business records.

3
00:00:17.905 --> 00:00:26.715
Logging and diagnostics should help engineers operate the system without turning logs into a second copy of sensitive data.

4
00:00:26.715 --> 00:00:28.935
Uploads need explicit controls.

5
00:00:28.935 --> 00:00:40.824
Define size limits, type expectations, storage rules, retention rules, malware scanning where applicable, review workflow, quarantine behavior, and deletion behavior.

6
00:00:40.824 --> 00:00:46.411
Do not trust file names, content types, extensions, or metadata by themselves.

7
00:00:46.411 --> 00:00:52.140
Treat those values as hints that may be wrong, stale, or supplied by the sender.

8
00:00:52.140 --> 00:01:07.396
Logs should capture safe context such as workflow name, correlation ID, partner or tenant identifier where appropriate, parser result, validation result, file size category, failure category, and processing stage.

9
00:01:07.396 --> 00:01:19.929
Avoid routine logging of raw XML, credentials, tokens, private keys, connection strings, local file paths, internal URLs, or full exception details without a redaction review.

10
00:01:19.929 --> 00:01:25.015
Development diagnostics should not silently become production behavior.

11
00:01:25.015 --> 00:01:33.752
A verbose parser exception that is useful during development may be inappropriate in a user response or shared log stream.

12
00:01:33.752 --> 00:01:42.848
Production behavior should separate user-facing messages, operator logs, secure debug captures, and incident response evidence.

13
00:01:42.848 --> 00:01:59.106
A production review should cover parser settings, DTD behavior, resolver behavior, schema sources, XPath patterns, XSLT settings, signature verification, uploads, logging, monitoring, dependency versions, and incident response.

14
00:01:59.106 --> 00:02:04.263
That review should include both successful processing and failure modes.

15
00:02:04.263 --> 00:02:07.200
Monitoring is part of the control system.

16
00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:21.882
Repeated parser failures, oversized uploads, validation drift, transformation failures, signature verification failures, and unusual XML volume can all indicate broken integrations or reliability pressure.

17
00:02:21.882 --> 00:02:27.039
Safe XML operations depend on signals that engineers can see and act on.
